      "id": "745286b3-b77a-43a8-9fc1-e5b99cfdbe2e",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Perfect — this section is the **final step in the nesting ladder**.\nYou’ve now seen **all 5 ways Java creates behavior objects**, and this code intentionally puts them **side-by-side** so you can compare them.\n\nI’ll explain **what’s new**, then **walk through `RunMethods` line-by-line**, because **that file is the core lesson**.\n\n---\n\n# 1️⃣ What is NEW in Section 13.6?\n\nThis section introduces:\n\n## ✅ **Anonymous Classes**\n\n> A class **without a name**, created **inline**, used **once**, and discarded.\n\nYou already know:\n\n* Static nested classes\n* Inner classes\n* Local classes\n\nNow you learn:\n\n* **Anonymous class**\n* And how it naturally leads to **lambdas**\n\n---\n\n# 2️⃣ The Big Teaching Goal of `RunMethods.java`\n\nThis file answers **one question**:\n\n> “How many ways can I provide a `Comparator`?”\n\nJava’s answer:\n\n1. Top-level class\n2. Static nested class\n3. Inner class\n4. Local class\n5. **Anonymous class**\n6. **Lambda expression**\n\nAnd the code uses **all six**.\n\n---\n\n# 3️⃣ The Setup: Data to sort\n\n```java\nList<StoreEmployee> storeEmployees = new ArrayList<>(List.of(...));\n```\n\nNothing special — just data to test sorting strategies.\n\n---\n\n# 4️⃣ The First Three (you already know these)\n\n### 🔹 `c0` – Top-level class\n\n```java\nvar c0 = new EmployeeComparator<StoreEmployee>();\n```\n\n📌 Defined in its own `.java` file\n📌 Old-school, reusable, verbose\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 `c1` – Static nested class\n\n```java\nvar c1 = new Employee.EmployeeComparator<StoreEmployee>();\n```\n\n📌 Belongs logically to `Employee`\n📌 No instance of `Employee` required\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 `c2` – Inner class\n\n```java\nvar c2 = new StoreEmployee().new StoreComparator<StoreEmployee>();\n```\n\n📌 Needs a `StoreEmployee` instance\n📌 Can access outer class fields (`store`)\n\n---\n\n# 5️⃣ Local Class (Revision)\n\n```java\nclass NameSort<T> implements Comparator<StoreEmployee> {\n    @Override\n    public int compare(StoreEmployee o1, StoreEmployee o2) {\n        return o1.getName().compareTo(o2.getName());\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```java\nvar c3 = new NameSort<StoreEmployee>();\n```\n\n📌 Exists **only inside `main`**\n📌 Named\n📌 Reusable **within the method**\n\nThis is useful when:\n\n* Logic is complex\n* Used multiple times in the same method\n\n---\n\n# 6️⃣ ⭐ Anonymous Class (THE NEW PART)\n\n```java\nvar c4 = new Comparator<StoreEmployee>() {\n    @Override\n    public int compare(StoreEmployee o1, StoreEmployee o2) {\n        return o1.getName().compareTo(o2.getName());\n    }\n};\n```\n\nLet’s break this down carefully.\n\n---\n\n## What is happening here?\n\nYou are saying:\n\n> “Create a class that implements `Comparator<StoreEmployee>`,\n> override `compare`,\n> create an object of it,\n> and **don’t bother naming the class**.”\n\n### That’s why it’s called **anonymous**\n\n---\n\n## Why no class name?\n\nBecause:\n\n* You’ll never reuse it\n* You don’t care about its identity\n* You only need the behavior **right here**\n\n---\n\n## Compiler view (mentally)\n\nThe compiler secretly turns this into something like:\n\n```java\nclass RunMethods$1 implements Comparator<StoreEmployee> {\n    public int compare(...) { ... }\n}\n```\n\nYou just don’t see it.\n\n---\n\n## Key rules of Anonymous Classes\n\n✅ Can:\n\n* Implement **one interface**\n* Extend **one class**\n* Override methods\n\n❌ Cannot:\n\n* Have constructors\n* Have a name\n* Be reused\n* Have multiple inheritance\n\n---\n\n# 7️⃣ The Final Evolution: Lambda\n\n```java\nsortIt(storeEmployees,\n    (o1, o2) -> o1.getName().compareTo(o2.getName())\n);\n```\n\nThis works because:\n\n* `Comparator` is a **functional interface**\n* It has **exactly one abstract method**\n* Java can infer everything else\n\n### Anonymous class → Lambda rule\n\nIf:\n\n* You have an anonymous class\n* That implements a **functional interface**\n* And overrides **one method**\n\n👉 You can replace it with a **lambda**\n\n---\n\n# 8️⃣ Why `sortIt` is written this way\n\n```java\npublic static <T> void sortIt(\n    List<T> list,\n    Comparator<? super T> comparator\n)\n```\n\n### Why `? super T`?\n\nBecause:\n\n* You can pass a comparator of `Employee`\n* To sort `StoreEmployee`\n\nThis is **PECS**:\n\n> Producer Extends, Consumer Super\n\nComparator **consumes** `T`, so → `super`\n\n---\n\n# 9️⃣ Output line that matters\n\n```java\nSystem.out.println(\"Sorting with Comparator: \" + comparator.toString());\n```\n\nThis helps you see:\n\n* Different comparator **instances**\n* Different generated class names (especially anonymous ones)\n\n---\n\n# 🔟 How this ties back to previous sections\n\n| Technique     | Named | Reusable | Scope      |\n| ------------- | ----- | -------- | ---------- |\n| Top-level     | ✅     | ✅        | App        |\n| Static nested | ✅     | ✅        | App        |\n| Inner         | ✅     | ✅        | Object     |\n| Local         | ✅     | ❌        | Method     |\n| **Anonymous** | ❌     | ❌        | Expression |\n| **Lambda**    | ❌     | ❌        | Expression |\n\n---\n\n# 🔑 Interview-grade takeaway\n\n> **Anonymous classes are one-off implementations of interfaces or classes, mainly used before Java 8 and now mostly replaced by lambdas.**\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Mental model (VERY IMPORTANT)\n\nThink of it like this:\n\n```\nTop-level class   → reusable tool\nNested class      → tool owned by a class\nLocal class       → tool owned by a method\nAnonymous class   → disposable tool\nLambda            → behavior, not a tool\n```\n\n---\n\nIf you want next:\n\n* 🔁 Rewrite anonymous class → lambda step-by-step\n* 🧪 Bytecode-level explanation (how JVM sees these)\n* ❓ When anonymous classes are still better than lambdas\n\nJust say 👍\n",
